/*  The parts you wire together.

    A gate is a small record of WIRE NAMES — never of values. That is the whole idea: the
    gate says "read these two lines, write that one", and it says the same thing whatever is
    currently on the lines. The values live in one place, the wire map, and a gate is a rule
    for getting from it to itself.

    ★ Six kinds, as a closed union. `Judge` over it is checked for coverage by the compiler,
    so adding a seventh gate makes every walk below fail to compile until it is handled —
    which is what you want from a change that adds a way for a circuit to behave. ★ That is
    not hypothetical: `nand-gate` WAS added after the rest of this program worked, and the
    compiler listed precisely the two places that had to learn about it.

    ★★ The arities differ, and that is why the union is the right shape rather than one
    `gate` object with a `kind` field. A NOT gate has one input and a tri-state buffer has
    two-with-different-jobs; giving them a shared shape would mean a `right` field that is
    meaningless for half of them, and every reader wondering what goes in it.
*/

Define object and-gate with (the text left, the text right, the text out).
Define object or-gate  with (the text left, the text right, the text out).
Define object xor-gate with (the text left, the text right, the text out).
Define object not-gate with (the text source, the text out).

/*  NAND — and the reason it is a PRIMITIVE here rather than an AND wired into a NOT.

    ⚠⚠ Those two are not the same circuit, and finding out why was the sharpest thing this
    program taught. An AND feeding a NOT has an INTERNAL WIRE between them; a NAND does not.
    In a latch, that difference is the whole device: the latch's state lives on its outputs,
    and a decomposed NAND puts a second, hidden state node in the loop that nobody seeds.
    Started from rest that extra wire is floating, and on the first round it overwrites the
    very value the latch was supposed to be holding — so a latch built from AND plus NOT
    cannot remember anything, and prints x where it should print what it was given.

    ★ Real logic families have the same fact the other way round: NAND is the cheap primitive
    and AND is a NAND followed by an inverter, not the reverse. */
Define object nand-gate with (the text left, the text right, the text out).

/*  A tri-state buffer — the part that makes `z` mean something.

    When `enable` is high it passes `source` through. When `enable` is low it lets go of the
    line entirely and drives `z`, so somebody else may drive it instead. That is how a bus
    works: many drivers, all but one parked.

    ⚠ An enable that is itself x or z gives x, not z. The buffer cannot know whether it is
    supposed to be driving, so it cannot promise the line is free.
*/
Define object tri-gate with (the text source, the text enable, the text out).

// ── Reading a wire ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ★ A name nobody has written is FLOATING, not unknown, and the difference matters. `z` says
// "no driver", which is the honest state of a wire that exists only because a gate mentions
// it. Defaulting to `x` would mean "somebody drove this and we don't know what to", which is
// a different claim and would make every unconnected input contend with its real driver.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to on-wire, given (
        the map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) wires,
        the text name):
    Return the entry for name in wires but void is a new floating { }.
Done.

// ── What a gate drives, and where ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two walks rather than one returning a pair: the wire a gate writes is a property of the
// gate alone, while the value needs the whole circuit's current state. Keeping them apart
// means the settling loop can ask "which wires does this circuit drive?" without evaluating
// anything, which is what it needs to tell a driven wire from a primary input.

Bind text to driven-wire, given (
        the (and-gate or or-gate or xor-gate or nand-gate or not-gate or tri-gate) part):
    Judge part, where it is:
        An and-gate, return the out of it.
        An or-gate,  return the out of it.
        A xor-gate,  return the out of it.
        A nand-gate, return the out of it.
        A not-gate,  return the out of it.
        A tri-gate,  return the out of it.
    Done.
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to driven-value, given (
        the (and-gate or or-gate or xor-gate or nand-gate or not-gate or tri-gate) part,
        the map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) wires):
    Judge part, where it is:
        An and-gate:
            Define left as cast on-wire on (wires, the left of it).
            Define right as cast on-wire on (wires, the right of it).
            Return cast both-of on (left, right).
        Done.
        An or-gate:
            Define left as cast on-wire on (wires, the left of it).
            Define right as cast on-wire on (wires, the right of it).
            Return cast either-of on (left, right).
        Done.
        A xor-gate:
            Define left as cast on-wire on (wires, the left of it).
            Define right as cast on-wire on (wires, the right of it).
            Return cast differing on (left, right).
        Done.
        A nand-gate:
            Define left as cast on-wire on (wires, the left of it).
            Define right as cast on-wire on (wires, the right of it).
            Return cast inverted on (cast both-of on (left, right)).
        Done.
        A not-gate:
            Return cast inverted on (cast on-wire on (wires, the source of it)).
        Done.
        A tri-gate:
            Define enabled as cast on-wire on (wires, the enable of it).
            Define carried as cast on-wire on (wires, the source of it).
            Judge enabled, where it is:
                A high, return carried.
                A low, return a new floating { }.
                An unknown, return a new unknown { }.
                A floating, return a new unknown { }.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
